<DocsLayout title="Queue and delivery" description="Understand RelayBridge durable local SMTP acceptance, queue persistence, retries, Microsoft token acquisition, TLS, XOAUTH2, and Exchange acceptance semantics." section="Operations">
  <section>
    <h2>Two separate SMTP connections</h2>
    <pre><code>device or application
  → private-LAN SMTP listener
  → streamed incoming spool
  → durable flush and pending promotion
  → SQLite queue metadata commit
  → local SMTP 250
  → bounded queue claim and Microsoft token
  → STARTTLS + XOAUTH2 to smtp.office365.com:587
  → recipients, original MIME payload, final Exchange response</code></pre>
    <p>The device-facing connection and Microsoft-facing connection are independent. A legacy device does not gain OAuth or TLS capability; RelayBridge owns a separate modern connection upstream.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What durable local acceptance means</h2>
    <p>During SMTP DATA, RelayBridge streams the original RFC 5322/MIME payload into <code>spool\incoming</code>, durably flushes it, atomically promotes it to <code>spool\pending</code>, commits SQLite metadata, and only then returns local SMTP <code>250</code>.</p>
    <p>That response means RelayBridge owns a durable copy on this Windows host. Queue data and messages survive a normal service restart or Windows reboot. It does not mean Exchange Online or a recipient inbox has accepted the message.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Microsoft delivery attempt</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li><strong>Validate current configuration and sender.</strong>The worker refuses to deliver through an invalid or unready Microsoft configuration.</li>
      <li><strong>Acquire an application token.</strong>The Windows service uses the customer-owned Entra application and local certificate private key.</li>
      <li><strong>Connect to Exchange Online.</strong>RelayBridge uses the fixed SMTP endpoint on TCP 587.</li>
      <li><strong>Require STARTTLS.</strong>Normal platform certificate and hostname validation must succeed.</li>
      <li><strong>Authenticate with XOAUTH2.</strong>RelayBridge does not use Basic Auth and does not fall back to Graph mail delivery.</li>
      <li><strong>Submit all recipients and the original payload.</strong>The worker streams MIME with SMTP dot-stuffing and waits for the final Exchange response.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Acceptance, retries, and duplicates</h2>
    <p>Only the final Exchange <code>250</code> marks the queue item delivered. It proves Microsoft accepted the SMTP transaction; it does not guarantee inbox placement, downstream delivery, or the absence of later Microsoft filtering or policy handling.</p>
    <p>The worker uses bounded concurrency, retry delay with jitter, maximum attempt and age policies, and permanent-failure classification. Startup reconciliation repairs safe database/spool inconsistencies before work resumes. Successfully delivered payloads are deleted under the default policy.</p>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <strong>Delivery is at-least-once</strong>
      <p>If a connection fails after the DATA terminator but before RelayBridge receives Exchange's final response, the result is ambiguous. RelayBridge may retry, so duplicates remain possible. Exactly-once delivery is not promised.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

